To create a new line within a cell in LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. Here is an example:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
```

However, if you want to keep the `\\` command within a cell, without splitting it across multiple cells, you can use the `array` package and its `m{}` column type, which allows for line breaks within a cell. Here is an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{array}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|m{5cm}|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `p{}` column type from the `array` package, but it requires a fixed height for the cell, which might not be what you want.

Another option is to use the `longtable` package, which allows for line breaks within a cell and also provides a way to continue a table over multiple pages.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{longtable}

\begin{document}

\begin{longtable}{|p{5cm}|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{longtable}

\end{document}
```

Note that you can adjust the `5cm` value to suit your needs.

If you want to have more control over the table and its alignment, you can use the `booktabs` package, which provides a more professional-looking table layout. However, it does not provide a built-in way to break lines within a cell.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{booktabs}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|}
\toprule
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

You can achieve this in C++ using template metaprogramming and the `std::tuple` class from the `<tuple>` header. However, a more straightforward and concise way is to use a function template with parameter pack expansion. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

template <typename... Args>
void print(Args... args) {
    ((std::cout << args << " "), ...);
    std::cout << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    float y = 3.14;
    char c = 'a';

    print(x, y, c);  // prints: 5 3.14 a
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the `print` function takes a variable number of arguments using parameter pack expansion (`Args... args`). The `(std::cout << args << " ", ...)` expression uses a fold expression to print each argument followed by a space, and the final `std::endl` is printed after all arguments have been processed.

This approach is concise, readable, and efficient. It allows you to write a single function that can handle any number of arguments, making it a great solution for your use case.

Alternatively, you can use a `std::apply` function from `<utility>` header to achieve the same result:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

template <typename... Args>
void print(Args... args) {
    std::apply([](auto... xs) { ((std::cout << xs << " "), ...); std::cout << std::endl; }, std::make_tuple(args...));
}

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    float y = 3.14;
    char c = 'a';

    print(x, y, c);  // prints: 5 3.14 a
    return 0;
}
```

In this version, `std::apply` applies the lambda function to the elements of the tuple created by `std::make_tuple`.

